,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218465073,"data":"Okay, so I need to find the number of positive integers n between 1 and 100 such that the polynomial x^{2n} + 1 + (x + 1)^{2n} is divisible by x² + x + 1. Hmm, divisibility of polynomials. I remember that if a polynomial P(x) is divisible by another polynomial Q(x), then all the roots of Q(x) must be roots of P(x). So, maybe I should start by finding the roots of x² + x + 1 and then check when those roots satisfy the equation P(r) = 0, where P(x) is the given polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.21848825,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218493118,"data":"First, let me recall that the roots of x² + x + 1 are the complex cube roots of unity except for 1. Let me denote one of them as ω. So ω satisfies ω² + ω + 1 = 0, and ω³ = 1. That's right, because the minimal polynomial for ω is x² + x + 1, and since ω³ - 1 = (ω - 1)(ω² + ω + 1), so ω³ = 1. So ω is a primitive 3rd root of unity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218499218,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218503505,"data":"Therefore, if x² + x + 1 divides P(x) = x^{2n} + 1 + (x + 1)^{2n}, then substituting x = ω \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.21850768,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.21851198,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218516018,"data":"===== pidx 1 · n_tok 1024 · 완주 False · 마커 0 =====\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218519888,"data":"Okay, so I need to solve this problem about a polynomial p(x) that satisfies two conditions: first, p(1) = 210, and second, for all real numbers x, the equation (x + 10)p(2x) = 8(x - 4)p(x + 6) holds. The goal is to find p(10). Alright, let's start breaking this down step by step.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218524353,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218528975,"data":"First, since p(x) is a polynomial, that gives us some structure to work with. Polynomials have finite degrees, and their behavior is determined by their coefficients and degree. The functional equation given relates the value of the polynomial at 2x to its value at x + 6, scaled by some factors involving x. Maybe I can figure out the degree of the polynomial first?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218533722,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218537327,"data":"Let me denote the degree of p(x) as n. Then, let's analyze the degrees of both sides of the equation (x + 10)p(2x) = 8(x - 4)p(x + 6).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218541253,"data":"\n"}
