,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218654334,"data":"Okay, so I need to find the number of pairs of complex numbers (z₁, z₂) that satisfy three conditions: their product z₁z₂ is pure imaginary, their quotient z₁/z₂ is real, and both have absolute value 1. Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218658922,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218662514,"data":"First, since both z₁ and z₂ are complex numbers with |z₁| = |z₂| = 1, they lie on the unit circle in the complex plane. That means I can represent them in the form z = e^{iθ}, where θ is the angle (argument) of the complex number. So maybe I can write z₁ = e^{iα} and z₂ = e^{iβ}, where α and β are real numbers between 0 and 2π, right?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218667605,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218671164,"data":"Let me note that down:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.21867796,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218685843,"data":"Let z₁ = e^{iα} and z₂ = e^{iβ}, with α, β ∈ [0, 2π).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218703056,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218707933,"data":"Now, the first condition is that z₁z₂ is pure imaginary. Let's compute z₁z₂:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218712543,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218716803,"data":"z₁z₂ = e^{iα} * e^{iβ} = e^{i(α + β)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":651.218721325,"data":"\n"}
